2.5 — Verify the reconstructed context is correct

Check the event log:

powershell
curl http://localhost:8000/api/runs/47/events | jq .

Expected log structure:

json
[
  {"seq": 1, "type": "RUN_SUBMITTED", "payload": {...}},
  {"seq": 2, "type": "RUN_CLAIMED", "payload": {"worker": "worker-a#3", "epoch": 1}},
  {"seq": 3, "type": "STEP_STARTED", "payload": {"step_index": 0}},
  {"seq": 4, "type": "TOOL_INTENT", "payload": {"tool": "search_professors", ...}},
  {"seq": 5, "type": "TOOL_RESULT", "payload": {"tool": "search_professors", "result": [...]}}},
  {"seq": 6, "type": "STEP_COMPLETED", "payload": {"step_index": 0}},
  {"seq": 7, "type": "RUN_CLAIMED", "payload": {"worker": "worker-b#1", "epoch": 2}},
  // ← After this point, Worker B resumes. The previous TOOL_INTENT is NOT repeated
  {"seq": 8, "type": "STEP_STARTED", "payload": {"step_index": 1}},
  {"seq": 9, "type": "TOOL_INTENT", "payload": {"tool": "fetch_page", ...}},
  // ... rest of execution
  {"seq": N, "type": "RUN_COMPLETED", "payload": {...}}
]

Key observation: The event log shows exactly one TOOL_RESULT for each tool call — no duplicates. Worker B didn't re-run the search from step 0; it picked up at step 1. That's the reconstruction working.

